    q: "What is a VPC? What is the difference between public and private subnets?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "A VPC is my own isolated network in AWS with a CIDR range like 10.0.0.0/16, split into subnets that each live in one AZ. A public subnet has a route to an Internet Gateway, so resources with public IPs are reachable from the internet. A private subnet has no such route, so I put databases and app servers there and only load balancers in public subnets.",
    code: "VPC_ID=$(aws ec2 create-vpc --cidr-block 10.0.0.0/16 \\\n    --query 'Vpc.VpcId' --output text)\n\n# Public subnet: gets a route to the Internet Gateway (load balancers)\naws ec2 create-subnet --vpc-id $VPC_ID --cidr-block 10.0.1.0/24 --availability-zone us-east-1a\n# Private subnet: no internet route (databases, app servers)\naws ec2 create-subnet --vpc-id $VPC_ID --cidr-block 10.0.11.0/24 --availability-zone us-east-1a\n\naws ec2 describe-subnets --filters Name=vpc-id,Values=$VPC_ID \\\n    --query 'Subnets[].{Id:SubnetId,Cidr:CidrBlock,AZ:AvailabilityZone}' --output table",
