    q: "What are route tables, an Internet Gateway and a NAT Gateway?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "A route table decides where traffic from a subnet goes. An Internet Gateway allows two-way internet traffic for public subnets, and a NAT Gateway sits in a public subnet and lets private instances make outbound calls, like downloading updates, without being reachable from outside. NAT Gateways cost money per hour and per GB, so for S3 and DynamoDB traffic I add free gateway VPC endpoints instead.",
    code: "# Public route table: internet traffic -> Internet Gateway\naws ec2 create-route --route-table-id rtb-0public1234567890 \\\n    --destination-cidr-block 0.0.0.0/0 --gateway-id igw-0abc123def4567890\n\n# Private route table: outbound-only internet -> NAT Gateway\naws ec2 create-route --route-table-id rtb-0private123456789 \\\n    --destination-cidr-block 0.0.0.0/0 --nat-gateway-id nat-0abc123def4567890\n\naws ec2 describe-route-tables --route-table-ids rtb-0private123456789 \\\n    --query 'RouteTables[0].Routes'",
