    q: "What is an Elastic Load Balancer? ALB vs NLB?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "A load balancer spreads traffic across healthy targets in multiple AZs and stops sending to targets that fail health checks. An Application Load Balancer works at layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS) and can route by path or host, like /api to one service and / to another. A Network Load Balancer works at layer 4 (TCP/UDP), handles millions of requests with very low latency and supports static IPs. For web apps and APIs I use an ALB with an ACM certificate on the HTTPS listener.",
    code: "aws elbv2 create-load-balancer --name web-alb --type application \\\n    --scheme internet-facing \\\n    --subnets subnet-0aa11bb22cc33dd44 subnet-0bb22cc33dd44ee55 \\\n    --security-groups sg-0123456789abcdef0 \\\n    --query 'LoadBalancers[0].{Arn:LoadBalancerArn,DNS:DNSName,Type:Type,State:State.Code}'\n\n# Route /api/* to the API target group\naws elbv2 create-rule --listener-arn $LISTENER_ARN --priority 10 \\\n    --conditions Field=path-pattern,Values='/api/*' \\\n    --actions Type=forward,TargetGroupArn=$API_TG_ARN",
