    q: "How do DynamoDB keys work, and what is the difference between Query and Scan?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "Every item has a partition key, which decides which partition stores it, and optionally a sort key, which orders items inside that partition. Query reads only the items for one partition key, optionally filtered by a sort key condition, so it's fast and cheap. Scan reads the whole table and filters afterwards, so it gets slower and costlier as the table grows. I design keys around my access patterns, like pk=USER#42 and sk=ORDER#date, so every common read is a Query.",
    code: "import boto3\nfrom boto3.dynamodb.conditions import Key, Attr\n\ntable = boto3.resource(\"dynamodb\").Table(\"app-table\")\n# Query: reads only the items in user 42's partition\nq = table.query(KeyConditionExpression=Key(\"pk\").eq(\"USER#42\"))\n# Scan: reads EVERY item in the table, then filters\ns = table.scan(FilterExpression=Attr(\"pk\").eq(\"USER#42\"))\nprint(\"Query  Count:\", q[\"Count\"], \"ScannedCount:\", q[\"ScannedCount\"])\nprint(\"Scan   Count:\", s[\"Count\"], \"ScannedCount:\", s[\"ScannedCount\"])",
    output: "Query  Count: 3 ScannedCount: 3\nScan   Count: 3 ScannedCount: 50000",
