    q: "What is a Lambda authorizer in API Gateway?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "A Lambda authorizer is a function API Gateway calls before my backend: it gets the token or request headers and returns an IAM policy that allows or denies execute-api:Invoke, and it can also return a context object that is passed to the backend. Raising 'Unauthorized' returns 401 and a Deny policy returns 403. I enable result caching (by token, for a few minutes) so the authorizer doesn't run on every request, and if I only need standard JWT checks I use the built-in Cognito or JWT authorizer instead.",
    code: "TEAMS = {\"Bearer tok-orders-7f3a\": \"team-orders\"}   # real code verifies a JWT instead\n\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    token = event.get(\"authorizationToken\")\n    if not token:\n        raise Exception(\"Unauthorized\")             # -> 401\n    team = TEAMS.get(token)\n    effect = \"Allow\" if team else \"Deny\"            # Deny -> 403\n    return {\"principalId\": team or \"unknown\",\n            \"policyDocument\": {\"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": [{\n                \"Action\": \"execute-api:Invoke\", \"Effect\": effect, \"Resource\": event[\"methodArn\"]}]}}",
