    q: "What are VPC endpoints and when do you need them?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "VPC endpoints let resources in private subnets reach AWS services without going through a NAT Gateway or the internet. Gateway endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB, are free and work through route tables; interface endpoints (PrivateLink) put an ENI with a private IP in my subnets for services like Secrets Manager, ECR or SQS and cost per hour and per GB. Besides security, the S3 gateway endpoint often saves real money on NAT data processing charges, and I can lock a bucket to the endpoint with the aws:SourceVpce condition.",
    code: "# Gateway endpoint for S3: free, added to a route table\naws ec2 create-vpc-endpoint --vpc-id vpc-0a1b2c3d4e5f67890 \\\n    --service-name com.amazonaws.us-east-1.s3 \\\n    --route-table-ids rtb-0123456789abcdef0 --query 'VpcEndpoint.VpcEndpointId'\n\n# Interface endpoint (PrivateLink) for Secrets Manager: an ENI in my subnet\naws ec2 create-vpc-endpoint --vpc-id vpc-0a1b2c3d4e5f67890 \\\n    --vpc-endpoint-type Interface --service-name com.amazonaws.us-east-1.secretsmanager \\\n    --subnet-ids subnet-0aa11bb22cc33dd44 --private-dns-enabled \\\n    --query 'VpcEndpoint.VpcEndpointId'",
