    q: "When would you use Kinesis Data Streams instead of SQS?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "SQS is a work queue: each message is processed by one consumer and deleted, it scales automatically and is the simple default for background jobs. Kinesis Data Streams is an ordered, replayable log: records are kept for 24 hours up to 365 days, ordered per partition key within a shard, and several independent consumers can read the same data, like analytics and an archiver. I choose Kinesis for high-volume event or clickstream data that several consumers need or that I may replay, and SQS (FIFO if order matters) for tasks.",
    code: "# SQS: one job, one consumer, deleted after processing\naws sqs send-message \\\n    --queue-url https://sqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/resize-jobs \\\n    --message-body '{\"key\": \"uploads/cat.png\"}'\n\n# Kinesis: an event in an ordered, replayable stream\naws kinesis put-record --stream-name clickstream \\\n    --partition-key user-42 \\\n    --cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out \\\n    --data '{\"user\": \"user-42\", \"page\": \"/cart\", \"ts\": \"2026-11-12T10:51:03Z\"}'",
    output: "{\n    \"MD5OfMessageBody\": \"6f1e7d8b2c4a9e0f3b5d7c1a8e2f4b6d\",\n    \"MessageId\": \"d1f6c2a4-9b7e-4c3d-8a5f-0e2b1c7d9f3a\"\n}\n\n{\n    \"ShardId\": \"shardId-000000000001\",\n    \"SequenceNumber\": \"49657236105374821305621964731256487215340815746261090322\"\n}",
