    q: "What are your AWS security best practices? (KMS, WAF, Shield, GuardDuty)",
    level: 'Advanced',
    a: "I encrypt data at rest with KMS (customer managed keys with rotation for sensitive data) and in transit with TLS. At the edge, AWS WAF on CloudFront or the ALB blocks common attacks like SQL injection and bad bots with managed rule groups and rate limits, and Shield Standard gives free DDoS protection (Shield Advanced adds a response team and cost protection). GuardDuty detects threats like crypto-mining or leaked credentials from CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs and DNS logs. On top of that: least privilege, MFA, no long-lived keys, CloudTrail everywhere and Security Hub to see it all in one place.",
    code: "# KMS: customer managed key with automatic yearly rotation\nKEY_ID=$(aws kms create-key --description \"orders data\" \\\n    --query KeyMetadata.KeyId --output text)\naws kms enable-key-rotation --key-id $KEY_ID\n\n# Encrypt a small value (up to 4 KB) directly with the key\naws kms encrypt --key-id $KEY_ID --plaintext fileb://api-token.txt\n\n# GuardDuty: turn on threat detection in this region\naws guardduty create-detector --enable",
