    q: "How does cross-account access with assume-role work?",
    level: 'Advanced',
    a: "In the target account I create a role whose trust policy names the source account (or a specific role in it) as the principal, and attach the permissions it needs. A user or service in the source account then calls sts:AssumeRole and gets temporary credentials for the target account. This is how I give a CI pipeline or auditors access to prod without creating users there. For third parties I also add an ExternalId condition, and I use CLI profiles with role_arn so I don't handle the keys by hand.",
    code: "# Trust policy on role ReadOnlyAuditor in account 111122223333:\n#   \"Principal\": {\"AWS\": \"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:root\"}\naws sts assume-role --role-arn arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/ReadOnlyAuditor \\\n    --role-session-name audit-sam\n\n# Easier: a profile in ~/.aws/config, then use --profile prod-audit\n# [profile prod-audit]\n# role_arn = arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/ReadOnlyAuditor\n# source_profile = default",
