    q: "Design question: how would you build a URL shortener on AWS?",
    level: 'Advanced',
    a: "I'd use API Gateway (HTTP API) with Lambda and a DynamoDB table keyed by the short code: POST creates a code, and GET /{code} returns a 301 redirect, which is a single-key lookup that stays fast at any scale. I generate a short 7-character code from a hash of the URL and write it with a condition attribute_not_exists, so a collision is detected (and I retry with a new code) instead of overwriting someone's link. Reads vastly outnumber writes, so I put CloudFront in front to cache redirects at the edge, add a TTL attribute for expiring links and send click events asynchronously to Kinesis or SQS for analytics.",
    code: "import hashlib, json, boto3\ntable = boto3.resource(\"dynamodb\").Table(\"short-urls\")\n\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):  # HTTP API: POST /links and GET /{code}\n    if event[\"requestContext\"][\"http\"][\"method\"] == \"POST\":\n        url = json.loads(event[\"body\"])[\"url\"]\n        code = hashlib.sha256(url.encode()).hexdigest()[:7]\n        table.put_item(Item={\"code\": code, \"url\": url},\n                       ConditionExpression=\"attribute_not_exists(code)\")  # never overwrite\n        return {\"statusCode\": 201, \"body\": json.dumps({\"short\": f\"https://sho.rt/{code}\"})}\n    item = table.get_item(Key={\"code\": event[\"pathParameters\"][\"code\"]})[\"Item\"]\n    return {\"statusCode\": 301, \"headers\": {\"Location\": item[\"url\"]}}",
